#!/usr/bin/env python3
# RozeLynx native messaging host ("rozelynx_host").
#
# Speaks the stdio JSON protocol with the RozeLynx Features extension:
#   -> {"action": "list"}                    list installed browsers
#   <- {"browsers": [{"id","name","kind",   each entry found on PATH;
#                     "terminal"?}],          CLI entries carry the preferred
#      "terminals": [{"id","name"}]}          terminal id
#   -> {"action": "open", "id": ..., "url": ...}
#   <- {"ok": true} / {"ok": false, "error": ...}
#
# Browser/terminal tables live in browsers.sh next to this script
# (single source shared with the open-external scheme handler).
# All launches are logged to /tmp/rozelynx-open-external.log.
# BROWSER_FAKE=1 in the environment disables real launches (testing).
#
#   -> {"action": "verify-record", "sha256": <64 hex>, "id": ..., "name": ...}
#   <- {"ok": true} / {"ok": false, "error": ...}
#   Appends {sha256, id, name, at} to ~/.config/rozelynx/verified-addons.json
#   (CRX3 attestation records written by the CWS converter; consumed by the
#   extension's auto-update attestation gate).
#
#   -> {"action": "list-verified"}
#   <- {"verified": [{"sha256","id","name","at"}, ...]}
#   Returns the contents of verified-addons.json.
#
#   -> {"action": "install-xpi", "id": <addon-id>, "path": <absolute xpi path>,
#       "sha256": <64 hex>, "version": ..., "name": ...}
#   <- {"ok": true} / {"ok": false, "error": ...}
#   Copies the XPI into the default profile's extensions/ directory
#   (profile-dropped XPIs are picked up by Firefox at startup only) and
#   records the sha256 in verified-addons.json. The id must look like an
#   extension id (a-z0-9@.-).
#
#   -> {"action": "profiles"}
#   <- {"ok": true, "default": <dir path>, "profiles": [{"name", "path",
#      "dir", "running", "default"}, ...]}
#   Lists RozeLynx profiles. Display names come from the Profile Groups
#   store (~/.config/rozelynx/bytewheel/rozelynx/Profile Groups/<store>.sqlite,
#   the names the Profile Manager shows); running state is derived from each
#   profile directory's `lock` symlink (alive PID under /proc).
#
#   -> {"action": "open-rozelynx-profile", "profile": <abs profile dir>,
#       "url": ..., "iface": ..., "setDefault": true|false}
#   <- {"ok": true} / {"ok": false, "error": ...}
#   If setDefault && iface, merge `user_pref("rozelynx.netiface.profile", ..)`
#   into that profile's user.js (takes effect on that profile's next start),
#   then launch `rozelynx -profile <dir> --external-link-open <url>
#   [--external-link-iface <iface>]` detached. The D-Bus remote hands that
#   command line to the running instance of <dir> when one exists or starts
#   the profile fresh.
#
# set-prefs accepts an optional "profile" key (a profile dir, absolute or a
# directory name under the Rozelynx profile root) to target a specific
# profile instead of the default one.
#
#   -> {"action": "uget-probe"}
#   <- {"ok": true, "integrator": "uget-integrator", "version": ..., "uget": ...}
#   Asks uget-integrator for its version handshake without downloading
#   anything, so the extension can tell whether a uGet hand-off is possible
#   at all before it decides to intercept anything.
#
#   -> {"action": "uget", "url": <http(s) url>, "filename": ..., "referer": ...,
#       "fileSize": ..., "userAgent": ..., "cookies": <netscape cookie file>,
#       "clientVersion": ...}
#   <- {"ok": true, "via": "uget-integrator", "url": ..., "uget": ...}
#      / {"ok": false, "error": ...}
#   Hands ONE download off to uGet, driving the very same uget-integrator
#   stdio protocol the uGet Integration extension uses, so the queue, the
#   GUI and the cookie handling stay uGet's own. See do_uget().
#
# The two verbs above are uGet's, and stay uGet's: uget-integrator's protocol
# is a real integration with a real queue, not "run a program with a URL".
# Everything else the extension wants to hand a download to is served by the
# two GENERIC verbs below, which know nothing about any particular download
# manager - that is the point of them, and uGet is just the first entry in a
# list the user orders:
#
#   -> {"action": "handoff-probe", "program": "aria2c"}
#   <- {"ok": true, "program": "aria2c", "path": "/usr/bin/aria2c"}
#      / {"ok": false, "error": "aria2c not found on PATH"}
#   Resolves a program name on PATH without running it, so the extension can
#   tell the user which of their handlers can actually take a download.
#
#   -> {"action": "handoff", "program": "aria2c", "args": "-x 4 {url}",
#       "url": <http(s) url>, "filename": ..., "referer": ...,
#       "cookies": <netscape cookie file>, "userAgent": ...}
#   <- {"ok": true, "program": "aria2c", "argv": [...], "pid": 1234}
#      / {"ok": false, "error": ...}
#   Runs ONE program with the args template substituted and returns
#   immediately; it does not wait, because a download manager's window stays
#   open for as long as the user keeps it open. See do_handoff().
#
#   The args template may use {url}, {filename}, {referer}, {ua} and {cookies}.
#   {cookies} expands to the PATH of a 0600 file holding the Netscape-format
#   cookie text in the message, written by this host and removed when the child
#   exits - a program that wants cookies wants to open a file, and a file is the
#   only form aria2, wget and curl all agree on.
#
# TRUST BOUNDARY, deliberately bounded. This host's native-messaging manifest
# pins allowed_extensions to rozelynx-features@bytewheel.org, so no other
# extension can reach these verbs at all, and the program name always comes
# from a preference only the user or that extension can set. Within that, the
# generic verb execs a program by name with a LIST argv - never a shell, never
# a string - so nothing in a URL or a filename can become a second command.
# A program containing a path separator is refused rather than resolved
# relative to anything, and the resolved absolute path comes back in the
# reply so the log line says exactly what ran.

